\section{A pipeline of named steps}

Some pipelines are not pipelines of parsers.  A build script reads a
source file, generates code from it, compiles the code and runs the
tests; a data pipeline fetches rows, cleans them, aggregates them and
writes them out.  What these have in common is not the shape of the work
but the shape of the \emph{control}: a fixed sequence of named steps, each
of which may fail, and a decision about what a failure means --- stop
everything, or carry on and report at the end.

DSLtk provides a small machinery for exactly that, in about eighty lines
of the header between lines 7985 and 8058.  It consists of a task, a
chain of tasks, a shared state object that the tasks use to talk to one
another, and a policy that says what a failure means.  The design is
deliberately minimal, and in one respect --- the mechanism by which tasks
communicate --- it is minimal to the point of being stringly typed.

The chapter opens with the shipped API, because that is what a reader
needs in order to use the facility.  It then reports a serious defect:
in the shipped header, the runner and the conditional hook cannot be
instantiated at all, for a reason that is worth understanding because it
is a lesson about \code{std::variant}.  A complete working program then
follows, and it is a faithful mirror of the shipped machinery with the
single change that makes it usable.

\section{The shipped API}

\begin{table}[htbp]
