Two features of that table deserve comment before the defect is
discussed, because they describe the design.

The first is that the state map is the \emph{only} channel between
tasks.  A task receives a \code{TaskState&} and nothing else; it cannot
see its predecessors except by looking up their results in
\code{state.results} under their names.  This is stringly typed --- the
name is a \code{std::string} and a typo is a run-time failure --- but it
is also extremely flexible.  A task does not have to know what comes
before it in the chain; it only has to know the name of the task whose
output it wants.  The chain order and the data flow are two independent
things, and a tool that generates both can change one without the other.

The second is that the failure of a task is \emph{recorded, not
propagated}.  The \code{Result} returned by a task is stored in the state
map under the task's name, and the chain then decides what to do.  Under
\code{StopOnError} the chain breaks; under \code{ContinueOnError} it
carries on.  In either case the map holds the outcome of every task that
ran, so a partially completed chain is fully inspectable afterwards.
That is the design's real strength: a pipeline that fails half way
through still tells you exactly how far it got and what each step said.

\section{The runner does not instantiate}

The shipped machinery cannot be used as it stands, and the reason is
worth a careful statement because it is a subtle one.

\code{TaskState::results} has the type
\codel{std::unordered\_map<}{std::string,}\codel{Result<std::string,}{std::string>>}.
\code{dsl::Result<T, E>} stores a \code{std::variant<T, E>}.  With
\code{T} and \code{E} both \code{std::string}, that is a
\codel{std::variant<}{std::string, std::string>} --- a variant whose two
alternatives are the \emph{same} type.

The standard library cannot inspect such a variant by type.  The
requirement on \codel{std::holds\_}{alternative<T>} and on the
type-indexed \code{std::get<T>} is that the alternative appear exactly
once, and a static assertion enforces it.  The consequence is that
\code{Result<std::string, std::string>} can be \emph{created} --- via
\code{from_ok} and \code{from_err} --- but cannot be \emph{read}:
\code{is_ok()}, \code{is_err()}, \code{unwrap()} and \code{unwrap_or()}
are all ill-formed for that instantiation.

Three things in the shipped code depend on reading it, and all three
therefore fail to compile:

\begin{itemize}
\item \code{dsl::run} calls \code{result.is_err()} to decide whether to
  break out of the loop, so the runner cannot be instantiated.
\item \code{dsl::upon} uses \codel{state.results[}{task.name] = ...}, and
  \codel{std::unordered\_map::}{operator[]} requires its mapped type to be
  default-constructible.  \code{dsl::Result} declares a converting
  constructor, so it has no default constructor, and the subscript fails
  to compile as well.
\item Any user code that inspects \code{state.results} --- to print the
  map, to test a result, to read a payload --- fails to compile.
\end{itemize}

The shipped example \code{DSLtk/examples/21-task-pipeline.cpp} uses
exactly these constructs, and it does not compile against the shipped
header.  The intended usage looks like this:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={The intended use of the shipped task API.  The three lines marked \emph{ill-formed} do not compile, for the reason given in the text; the rest of the fragment does.},label={lst:ch24-intended}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

using Result = dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>;

auto read = dsl::Task{ "read", [] (dsl::TaskState &) {
    return Result::from_ok ("18");            // fine: the result can be made
} };
auto parse = dsl::Task{ "parse", [] (dsl::TaskState &state) {
    auto it = state.results.find ("read");
    if (it == state.results.end () || it->second.is_err ())   // ill-formed
      return Result::from_err ("missing source");
    return Result::from_ok (it->second.unwrap ());            // ill-formed
} };

auto chain = read | parse;                    // fine
dsl::TaskState state{};
dsl::run (state, chain);                      // ill-formed: calls is_err()
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{warnbox}[A defect in the shipped header]
\code{dsl::run}, \code{dsl::upon} and every inspection of
\code{TaskState::results} fail to compile, because
\code{Result<std::string, std::string>} is a
\code{std::variant} with two identical alternatives and therefore cannot
be read.  The example file \code{DSLtk/examples/21-task-pipeline.cpp}
does not compile against the shipped header either. Distinct payload types
solve type-based inspection, but map subscripting also requires a
default-constructible mapped type. The application-owned implementation below
uses a default-constructible outcome and \code{insert_or_assign}.
\end{warnbox}

A second, smaller gap deserves the same treatment.  \code{dsl::Result}
has no accessor for its error payload: there is no \code{err()}, no
\code{error()} and no \code{value_or()}, and \code{unwrap_or(fallback)}
returns a fallback of the \emph{value} type, not the error.  A
\code{Result} has no direct error accessor. For distinct payload types,
\code{map_err} exposes the error to its callable for inspection or
transformation. The example uses an application outcome with an explicit
message field to make reporting straightforward.

\section{A worked example}

Listing~\ref{lst:ch24-pipeline} is a complete program that implements a
three-stage ``read, parse, validate'' pipeline.  It keeps the shipped
shape --- a named job, a shared state map, a policy, a suspension flag,
the same insertion order and the same break conditions --- and changes
exactly two things: the outcome is a two-field struct rather than a
\code{dsl::Result}, and the job type is called \code{Job} rather than
\code{Task}.  Everything else, including the \code{operator|} overloads
and the \code{upon} hook, is a line-for-line mirror of the header.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcpp,caption={A complete three-stage pipeline: read, parse, validate, with both policies, a conditional job, and a suspension.},label={lst:ch24-pipeline}]
#include "DSLtk.hpp"

#include <algorithm>
#include <cctype>
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Why this program does not call dsl::run ()
//
//  dsl::TaskState::results has the type
//      std::unordered_map<std::string,
//                          dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>>
//  and dsl::Result<T, E> stores a std::variant<T, E>.  With T and E both
//  std::string that is a variant whose two alternatives are the *same*
//  type, and std::holds_alternative / std::get<T> are ill-formed for
//  such a variant.  Consequently dsl::Result<std::string, std::string>
//  can be created but never read: is_ok (), is_err (), unwrap () and
//  unwrap_or () cannot be instantiated.
//
//  dsl::run () calls result.is_err (), and dsl::upon () needs
//  unordered_map::operator[], which in turn needs a default-constructible
//  mapped type -- and dsl::Result has a user-declared constructor, so it
//  has no default constructor at all.  Neither function can therefore be
//  instantiated with the shipped TaskState.
//
//  The types below mirror the shipped machinery exactly -- a named job, a
//  shared state map keyed by name, a policy, a suspension flag, the same
//  insertion order and the same break conditions -- with one change: the
//  outcome is a two-field struct instead of dsl::Result, because
//  dsl::Result also never exposes its error payload.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

struct Outcome
{
  bool ok{ false };
  std::string text;
};

struct State
{
  std::unordered_map<std::string, Outcome> results;
  bool suspended{ false };
};

struct Job
{
  std::string name;
  std::function<Outcome (State &)> run_fn;
  Outcome
  run (State &state) const
  {
    return run_fn (state);
  }
};

struct Chain
{
  std::vector<Job> jobs;
  dsl::TaskPolicy policy{ dsl::TaskPolicy::StopOnError };
};

static Chain
operator| (Job lhs, Job rhs)
{
  return Chain{ { std::move (lhs), std::move (rhs) },
                dsl::TaskPolicy::StopOnError };
}

static Chain
operator| (Chain lhs, Job rhs)
{
  lhs.jobs.push_back (std::move (rhs));
  return lhs;
}

template <typename... Args>
static State &
run (State &state, const Chain &chain, Args &&...)
{
  for (const auto &job : chain.jobs)
    {
      auto result = job.run (state);
      state.results.insert_or_assign (job.name, result);
      if (!result.ok && chain.policy == dsl::TaskPolicy::StopOnError)
        break;
      if (state.suspended)
        break;
    }
  return state;
}

static void
suspend (State &state)
{
  state.suspended = true;
}

template <typename EventFn>
static void
upon (State &state, EventFn &&event, Job job)
{
  if (event ())
    state.results.insert_or_assign (job.name, job.run (state));
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  The three stages.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

static dsl::Parser<char>
digit ()
{
  return dsl::satisfy (
      [] (char c)
      { return std::isdigit (static_cast<unsigned char> (c)) != 0; },
      "digit");
}

static dsl::Parser<std::string>
digits ()
{
  auto core = dsl::operator& (digit (), dsl::operator* (digit ()));
  return dsl::parser (
      [core] (dsl::ParsecInput &in) -> dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>
      {
        auto r = core (in);
        if (!r)
          return dsl::ExpectedResult<std::string>::failure (
              r.error.pos, r.error.kind, r.error.expected);
        std::string s (1, (*r).first);
        for (char c : (*r).second)
          s += c;
        return s;
      });
}

static Job
read_job (std::string path)
{
  return Job{ "read",
              [path] (State &) -> Outcome
              {
                std::ifstream in (path, std::ios::in | std::ios::binary);
                if (!in)
                  return Outcome{ false,
                                  "cannot open '" + path + "'" };
                std::ostringstream ss;
                ss << in.rdbuf ();
                return Outcome{ true, ss.str () };
              } };
}

static Job
parse_job ()
{
  return Job{ "parse",
              [] (State &state) -> Outcome
              {
                auto it = state.results.find ("read");
                if (it == state.results.end ())
                  return Outcome{ false, "no input to parse" };
                if (!it->second.ok)
                  return Outcome{ false, "the input could not be read" };
                auto outcome
                    = dsl::run_parser (digits (), it->second.text);
                if (!outcome.value)
                  return Outcome{ false,
                                  "expected a digit at offset "
                                      + std::to_string (outcome.error.pos) };
                return Outcome{ true, *outcome.value };
              } };
}

static Job
validate_job ()
{
  return Job{ "validate",
              [] (State &state) -> Outcome
              {
                auto it = state.results.find ("parse");
                if (it == state.results.end ())
                  return Outcome{ false, "nothing to validate" };
                if (!it->second.ok)
                  return Outcome{ false, "the input did not parse" };
                long value = std::stol (it->second.text);
                if (value <= 0)
                  return Outcome{ false, "the value must be positive" };
                return Outcome{ true,
                                "value " + std::to_string (value)
                                    + " accepted" };
              } };
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Reporting.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

static void
dump (const char *label, const State &state)
{
  std::cout << "  " << label << ":\n";
  // The state map is an unordered_map, so the entries are sorted before
  // printing to make the output deterministic.
  std::vector<std::pair<std::string, Outcome>> entries (state.results.begin (),
                                                        state.results.end ());
  std::sort (entries.begin (), entries.end (),
             [] (const auto &a, const auto &b)
             { return a.first < b.first; });
  for (const auto &entry : entries)
    {
      std::cout << "    " << entry.first << " -> "
                << (entry.second.ok ? "ok" : "err") << "(\""
                << entry.second.text << "\")\n";
    }
  std::cout << "    suspended=" << (state.suspended ? "true" : "false")
            << "\n";
}

static void
write_file (const std::string &path, const std::string &text)
{
  std::ofstream out (path);
  out << text;
}

int
main ()
{
  write_file ("pipeline-good.txt", "42");
  write_file ("pipeline-bad.txt", "not a number");

  std::cout << "--- StopOnError, every stage succeeds ---\n";
  {
    Chain chain = read_job ("pipeline-good.txt") | parse_job ()
                  | validate_job ();
    State state;
    run (state, chain, 42, "an extra argument that is ignored");
    dump ("state", state);
  }

  std::cout << "\n--- StopOnError, the first stage fails ---\n";
  {
    Chain chain = read_job ("pipeline-missing.txt") | parse_job ()
                  | validate_job ();
    State state;
    run (state, chain);
    dump ("state", state);
  }

  std::cout << "\n--- ContinueOnError, the middle stage fails ---\n";
  {
    Chain chain = read_job ("pipeline-bad.txt") | parse_job ()
                  | validate_job ();
    chain.policy = dsl::TaskPolicy::ContinueOnError;
    State state;
    run (state, chain);
    dump ("state", state);
  }

  std::cout << "\n--- upon: a conditional job ---\n";
  {
    Chain chain = read_job ("pipeline-good.txt") | parse_job ()
                  | validate_job ();
    State state;
    run (state, chain);
    Job report{ "report",
                [] (State &) -> Outcome
                { return Outcome{ true, "all stages completed" }; } };
    upon (
        state,
        [&]
        {
          auto it = state.results.find ("validate");
          return it != state.results.end () && it->second.ok;
        },
        report);
    Job never{ "never",
               [] (State &) -> Outcome
               { return Outcome{ true, "should not run" }; } };
    upon (
        state,
        [&] { return state.results.count ("missing") != 0; }, never);
    dump ("state", state);
  }

  std::cout << "\n--- suspend: a job halts the chain ---\n";
  {
    Chain chain
        = read_job ("pipeline-good.txt")
          | Job{ "halt",
                 [] (State &state) -> Outcome
                 {
                   suspend (state);
                   return Outcome{ true, "halted" };
                 } }
          | validate_job ();
    State state;
    run (state, chain);
    dump ("state", state);
  }
  return 0;
}
\end{lstlisting}

The output is:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dslcmd,caption={Output of Listing~\ref{lst:ch24-pipeline}.},label={lst:ch24-pipeline-out}]
--- StopOnError, every stage succeeds ---
  state:
    parse -> ok("42")
    read -> ok("42")
    validate -> ok("value 42 accepted")
    suspended=false

--- StopOnError, the first stage fails ---
  state:
    read -> err("cannot open 'pipeline-missing.txt'")
    suspended=false

--- ContinueOnError, the middle stage fails ---
  state:
    parse -> err("expected a digit at offset 0")
    read -> ok("not a number")
    validate -> err("the input did not parse")
    suspended=false

--- upon: a conditional job ---
  state:
    parse -> ok("42")
    read -> ok("42")
    report -> ok("all stages completed")
    validate -> ok("value 42 accepted")
    suspended=false

--- suspend: a job halts the chain ---
  state:
    halt -> ok("halted")
    read -> ok("42")
    suspended=true
\end{lstlisting}

Four of the five scenarios are about the policy, and they show the
design's central property clearly.  In the second scenario the read stage
fails and the chain stops; the map contains one entry, and the two later
stages never ran, which is exactly the information a user needs to know
that the pipeline did not get past the first step.  In the third scenario
the policy is \code{ContinueOnError}, so the parse stage fails, the chain
carries on, and the validate stage runs --- and fails too, because it
correctly observes that its input did not parse.  The map now has three
entries, and the reader can see both that the chain continued and what
each step said.

The fourth scenario shows the \code{upon} hook.  The report job is
registered with a predicate that inspects the state map; because the
validate stage succeeded, the predicate is true and the report job runs,
and its result appears in the map alongside the chain's own results.  The
second registration in that scenario has a predicate that is never true,
and the job it names does not appear in the map at all --- which is the
point of a conditional hook.

The fifth scenario shows the suspension flag.  The \code{halt} job calls
\code{suspend} on the state, and the runner notices the flag after the
job has returned; the validate stage never runs, and the map shows
\code{suspended=true}.  Note the ordering: the suspended job's own
result \emph{is} recorded, so the map says both that the job ran and
that the chain stopped because of it.

\section{The design}

\subsection{Names as a protocol}

The state map is the whole interface between tasks, and it is keyed by
strings.  This is a deliberate trade, and it is worth naming both sides
of it.

On the bad side: a task that wants the output of the read stage writes
\code{state.results.find ("read")}, and a typo produces an empty
\code{optional} rather than a compile-time error.  There is no way to
declare that a task depends on another, so a tool that generates the
chain cannot check the dependencies either.

On the good side: the chain order and the data flow are independent.  A
task does not need to know where in the chain it sits, only which names
it may read.  The same task can be used in two different chains, in two
different positions, and the state map is the only thing that has to be
agreed upon.  The pipeline in Listing~\ref{lst:ch24-pipeline} is a
direct illustration: the validate job reads the name \code{"parse"} and
knows nothing about the read job, yet it works in the chain, and it
would keep working if the read job were replaced.

The same design explains the \code{upon} hook.  A conditional job is
registered against a predicate, not against a position in the chain; the
predicate typically inspects the state map, so the condition is expressed
in terms of the data rather than the order.

\subsection{Failure as data}

The second design decision is that failure is \emph{recorded} rather than
thrown or propagated.  A task returns an outcome; the runner stores it
under the task's name; the policy decides whether the chain continues.
Nothing is thrown, nothing unwinds, and nothing is lost.  A partially
completed chain is a first-class observable object: after the run, the
map says exactly which steps ran, in what state each finished, and the
suspension flag says whether the chain ended early by request.

This is a good fit for pipelines, which are usually reporting tools
rather than library calls.  A command-line tool that runs a build
pipeline and then prints a report wants precisely this: the set of steps
that completed, and the reason each failure occurred.  It is a poor fit
for a library function whose caller wants an exception, but the library
function can be written on top: the caller inspects the map and throws.

\subsection{Combining with the parser}

The parse stage of Listing~\ref{lst:ch24-pipeline} shows how the two
halves of this part of the book fit together, and the combination is
natural.  A task reads the raw text from the state map, runs a parser
over it with \code{dsl::run_parser}, and turns the outcome --- a value
or a \code{ParseError} --- into the pipeline's own outcome type.  The
parser contributes the position of the failure; the task contributes the
policy of what to do about it.

The one rule the combination must respect is that the two error channels
are different.  \code{dsl::ParseError} carries a position and a set of
labels; the pipeline's outcome carries a message.  A task that wants to
report a parse failure usefully must flatten the error into a string
before it can be stored --- and the flattening is lossy, because the
diagnostic formatter of Chapter~\ref{ch:parser-diagnostics} needs the
source text to compute a line and a column, and the source may no longer
be available by the time the task's outcome is inspected.

