    {policy : \texttt{StopOnError}};
\end{dslgraph}
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{A chain under the \code{StopOnError} policy.  The validate job
fails, the runner breaks out of the loop, and the report job never runs.
The state map records the outcome of every job that did run, and the
suspension flag stays false.}
\label{fig:ch24-cutoff}
\end{figure}

\begin{pseudocode}[Run a chain]
\textbf{function} run (state, chain, extra arguments \ldots)\\
\quad for each task in chain.tasks do\\
\qquad result := task.run (state)\\
\qquad state.results [task.name] := result\\
\qquad if result is an error and chain.policy = StopOnError then\\
\qquad\quad break\\
\qquad if state.suspended then\\
\qquad\quad break\\
\quad return state
\end{pseudocode}

The pseudocode is the shipped runner almost line for line, and two details
in it are easy to overlook.  The first is that the result is stored
\emph{before} the policy is consulted, so the failing task's own outcome
is always recorded.  The second is that the suspension flag is checked
after every task, including after the one that set it --- so a task that
suspends is allowed to finish and to record its result, and only the
tasks \emph{after} it are skipped.

\section{Edge cases and limits}

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{The shipped runner and hook do not compile.}  This is the
  central limitation of the facility as shipped, and it is discussed at
  length above.  The remedy is a distinct error type, or a user-defined
  outcome type.

\item \textbf{The state map is unordered.}  It is a
  \code{std::unordered_map}, so the iteration order of the results is
  unspecified and may differ between runs and between implementations.
  A report that iterates the map must sort it if it wants stable
  output; the listing does.

\item \textbf{Names are the only contract.}  A task that looks up a name
  that no task wrote finds nothing, and the difference between ``the
  input could not be read'' and ``there was no input'' is a run-time
  distinction that the type system cannot make.  A pipeline that must be
  robust against a missing predecessor has to check explicitly, as the
  parse and validate jobs of Listing~\ref{lst:ch24-pipeline} do.

\item \textbf{There is no cancellation other than the flag.}  The
  \code{suspended} member is a single boolean, and it is only consulted
  between tasks.  A long-running task cannot be interrupted; it can only
  ask not to be followed.

\item \textbf{The variadic arguments of \code{run} are ignored.}  The
  signature accepts any number of extra arguments and does nothing with
  them.  They are not forwarded to the tasks, and the tasks have no way to
  receive them.  The parameter exists, presumably, so that a caller may
  pass configuration without changing the runner's signature; in practice
  it is a trap for a reader who expects the arguments to mean something.

\item \textbf{\code{operator|} on a chain does not change the
  policy.}  \code{operator| (TaskChain, Task)} appends and returns the
  chain with the policy it already had.  A pipeline that is built as
  \code{a | b | c} therefore always has \code{StopOnError}, and the only
  way to change the policy is to assign to the member afterwards --- which
  is what the third scenario of Listing~\ref{lst:ch24-pipeline} does.

\item \textbf{\code{operator|} on two tasks always builds a chain with
  \code{StopOnError}.}  There is no overload that takes a policy, and no
  way to build a chain with \code{ContinueOnError} in a single
  expression.

\item \textbf{\code{upon} overwrites.}  It assigns to the state map
  under the task's name, so registering a job whose name collides with a
  chain job replaces that job's recorded outcome.  The shipped
  implementation uses the subscript operator, which also \emph{default
  constructs} the entry if it is absent --- and that is one of the
  reasons the shipped hook does not compile.

\item \textbf{The predicate of \code{upon} is called with no
  arguments.}  It cannot be told which task it is gating, and it cannot
  be handed the state; it must capture whatever it needs.  In the
  listing, the predicate captures the state by reference.

\item \textbf{There is no \code{TaskPipeline} machinery.}  The feature
  tag \code{dsl::TaskPipeline}, declared at line 7331 of the header, is
  an empty marker with an empty mixin, exactly like
  \code{dsl::CombinatorParser} of
  Chapter~\ref{ch:primitive-parsers}.  A derived \code{dsl::DSL} class
  that lists it as a feature gains nothing: the task types are
  free-standing and are not mixed in.  The tag is a placeholder for a
  future integration, not a working feature.

\item \textbf{No task is cancellable from outside.}  A caller cannot
  suspend a running chain, because \code{suspend} needs the
  \code{TaskState} and the runner holds it.  The flag is for tasks to set
  for themselves.

\item \textbf{Results are overwritten by name.}  Two tasks with the same
  name in the same chain fight over one map entry, and the last one to
  run wins.  Nothing checks that the names of a chain are distinct.
\end{itemize}

\section{Closing}

The task pipeline is a small, honest piece of design: a named step, a
shared map, a policy, and a flag.  Its two ideas --- that tasks
communicate by name rather than by argument, and that a failure is
recorded as data rather than thrown --- are the ideas that make a
pipeline inspectable after it has failed, which is the property that
matters most for a tool that reports.

Its implementation, in the shipped header, is nonetheless broken, and the
reason is instructive: a \code{std::variant} with two identical
alternatives cannot be read, and a type that stores a \code{Result} with
the same value and error types inherits that blindness.  A user who
wants the facility today must supply an outcome type of their own; the
listing in this chapter shows the smallest such repair and the semantics
it preserves.

The next part of the book turns to the other half of DSLtk's parsing
story: the PEG subsystem, where rules are compile-time pattern strings
rather than the combinator functions used here.
